00:22:19.230 --> 00:22:21.690
I might start washing my hands after I pee at home.

515
00:22:21.690 --> 00:22:22.610
I hope so.

516
00:22:24.470 --> 00:22:28.470
Well, now Mr. McGuire wants to make this golf game more interesting.

517
00:22:28.510 --> 00:22:31.850
So he suggests that they make a little wager.

518
00:22:32.010 --> 00:22:33.010
Why don't they do a little betting?

519
00:22:33.630 --> 00:22:34.690
Oh, my gosh.

520
00:22:34.830 --> 00:22:36.730
This is music to Kevin's ears.

521
00:22:36.930 --> 00:22:37.410
Kevin's like, what?

522
00:22:37.870 --> 00:22:40.070
Mr. McGuire suggests $10 a hole.

523
00:22:40.310 --> 00:22:41.210
But Kevin?

524
00:22:41.550 --> 00:22:43.710
Jenna, he lists all of these games.

525
00:22:43.990 --> 00:22:44.610
I know.

526
00:22:44.610 --> 00:22:45.510
I wrote them down.

527
00:22:45.630 --> 00:22:46.370
You want to hear what they are?

528
00:22:46.590 --> 00:22:47.330
I do.

529
00:22:47.330 --> 00:22:48.390
All right.

530
00:22:48.510 --> 00:22:54.590
He says, you want to play Skins, Acey Doocy, Bingo, Bango, Bongo, Sandies, Barkies, Wolf?

531
00:22:55.270 --> 00:22:55.710
Arnies.

532
00:22:55.890 --> 00:22:56.710
Don't forget Arnies.

533
00:22:57.010 --> 00:22:57.810
Oh, I'm so sorry.

534
00:22:57.930 --> 00:22:58.690
I forgot Arnies.

535
00:22:59.030 --> 00:22:59.370
Mm-hmm.

536
00:22:59.830 --> 00:23:00.710
I looked it up.

537
00:23:00.870 --> 00:23:03.610
These are all real ways to bet on golf.

538
00:23:04.070 --> 00:23:04.650
They're real.

539
00:23:04.850 --> 00:23:06.970
I mean, I have an explanation for all of them.

540
00:23:07.050 --> 00:23:07.970
I won't go into it.

541
00:23:08.090 --> 00:23:08.750
But fascinating.

542
00:23:08.970 --> 00:23:11.590
I didn't even realize you could do this much golf betting.

543
00:23:12.510 --> 00:23:14.550
Clearly, there's so much golf gambling.

544
00:23:14.750 --> 00:23:15.490
And you know what?

545
00:23:15.570 --> 00:23:16.990
It tracks that Kevin would know.

546
00:23:17.330 --> 00:23:19.670
Yeah, this is bringing up his whole gambling thing.

547
00:23:19.910 --> 00:23:20.310
Mm-hmm.

548
00:23:20.710 --> 00:23:25.790
And he admits that he's going to take the petty cash from Oscar and use it to turn it into

549
00:23:25.790 --> 00:23:26.450
next month's rent.

550
00:23:26.610 --> 00:23:29.610
So he is gambling with Dunder Mifflin's petty cash.

551
00:23:30.050 --> 00:23:32.730
Should we move on to this job fair, lady?

552
00:23:33.070 --> 00:23:35.010
Yes, let's move on to the job fair.

553
00:23:35.430 --> 00:23:38.070
Pam has to walk down memory lane for high school.

554
00:23:38.090 --> 00:23:39.690
And you learn a lot about her.

555
00:23:40.070 --> 00:23:44.850
Yeah, she has this talking head where she says this gym reminds her of all the times

556
00:23:44.850 --> 00:23:48.150
she pretended to have PMS to get out of playing sports.

557
00:23:48.350 --> 00:23:49.830
In particular, volleyball.

558
00:23:50.210 --> 00:23:53.050
But yet in company picnic, she's like a volleyball pro.

559
00:23:53.310 --> 00:23:53.990
So what's happening?

560
00:23:54.270 --> 00:23:56.710
Oh, have we ever gotten more mail?

561
00:23:58.510 --> 00:23:59.890
I feel seen.

562
00:24:00.110 --> 00:24:02.030
Thank you for everyone that wrote in.

563
00:24:02.370 --> 00:24:04.570
As soon as Pam said that, I was like, what the heck?

564
00:24:05.010 --> 00:24:05.450
I know.

565
00:24:05.750 --> 00:24:12.290
Chelsea B wrote in this theory on why it still tracks that she would have pretended to have

566
00:24:12.290 --> 00:24:15.070
PMS while also being a volleyball champion.

567
00:24:15.090 --> 00:24:16.730
And I loved it.

568
00:24:16.730 --> 00:24:18.730
So here is Chelsea's theory.

569
00:24:18.950 --> 00:24:24.510
She says, people always point out the continuity error that Pam says she faked cramps so she

570
00:24:24.510 --> 00:24:26.830
wouldn't have to play volleyball in gym class.

571
00:24:27.450 --> 00:24:32.850
However, maybe Pam faked cramps because she didn't want to have to play volleyball with

572
00:24:32.850 --> 00:24:35.290
her classmates who weren't very good at it.

573
00:24:35.510 --> 00:24:40.510
As a former athlete myself, I can tell you I wouldn't have had much fun playing my sport

574
00:24:40.510 --> 00:24:42.010
with people who sucked at it.

575
00:24:42.130 --> 00:24:45.630
So in my opinion, it's not surprising that she'd try to get out of it.

576
00:24:45.630 --> 00:24:48.050
Well, Chelsea, I think you're being very kind.

577
00:24:48.510 --> 00:24:49.030
What?

578
00:24:49.250 --> 00:24:56.790
I love that Chelsea is like, Pam is such a badass volleyball player that she cannot handle

579
00:24:56.790 --> 00:24:59.190
a gym class volleyball game.

580
00:24:59.390 --> 00:25:00.670
I mean, come on.

581
00:25:00.730 --> 00:25:04.070
In high school, like they would have pulled you out and put you in varsity volleyball.

582
00:25:04.490 --> 00:25:07.230
You would have been separated from the lame-os.

583
00:25:07.750 --> 00:25:09.390
No, this was gym class.

584
00:25:09.570 --> 00:25:12.830
This was just like everybody get exercise in gym class.

585
00:25:12.970 --> 00:25:14.210
We're going to play volleyball today.

586
00:25:14.210 --> 00:25:19.630
So Pam was probably on the varsity team, but she just didn't want to have to play in gym

587
00:25:19.630 --> 00:25:20.170
class.

588
00:25:20.410 --> 00:25:22.350
It was a waste of her time and talent.

589
00:25:22.950 --> 00:25:23.650
Okay, Chelsea.

590
00:25:23.950 --> 00:25:24.270
Okay.

591
00:25:25.310 --> 00:25:25.790
Okay.

592
00:25:26.330 --> 00:25:26.750
Okay.

593
00:25:27.310 --> 00:25:29.990
Well, I'm living for your piece of mail, Chelsea.

594
00:25:30.150 --> 00:25:31.090
I appreciated it.

595
00:25:31.270 --> 00:25:32.570
You made her day, Chelsea.

596
00:25:32.870 --> 00:25:36.570
Well, there's another deleted scene with Pam as she's going through the high school.

597
00:25:36.570 --> 00:25:43.150
She walks past the sports trophy case and there is a big old photo of Roy Anderson, the big

598
00:25:43.150 --> 00:25:44.870
jock, you know, in high school.

599
00:25:45.050 --> 00:25:48.210
And she looks at it and says, nine years of my life.

600
00:25:48.210 --> 00:25:49.950
I remember shooting that.

601
00:25:50.590 --> 00:25:53.690
They had done this little Photoshop of David Denman.

602
00:25:54.170 --> 00:25:54.730
Yeah.

603
00:25:55.530 --> 00:25:56.890
In a high school photo.

604
00:25:57.130 --> 00:26:02.550
And Oscar has a deleted talking head where he says, no, surprisingly enough, I did not

605
00:26:02.550 --> 00:26:05.250
always dream of being an accountant at Dunder Mifflin.

606
00:26:05.410 --> 00:26:07.590
In high school, I was just a normal kid.

607
00:26:07.590 --> 00:26:13.590
I made decent grades, played bass in a new wave band, had a poster of Kim Basinger over

608
00:26:13.590 --> 00:26:14.110
my bed.

609
00:26:14.390 --> 00:26:16.870
Oh, how I wanted to be Kim Basinger.

610
00:26:17.510 --> 00:26:19.550
That is a great talking head.

611
00:26:19.850 --> 00:26:20.750
It was.

612
00:26:20.930 --> 00:26:22.010
I wish it had stayed in.

613
00:26:22.630 --> 00:26:25.510
Michael is feeling pretty confident about his booth.

614
00:26:25.950 --> 00:26:30.490
So much so that the only thing that he brought was a blank piece of paper.

615
00:26:30.830 --> 00:26:35.110
So it's the four of them sitting at a table with a blank piece of paper in front of them.

616
00:26:35.110 --> 00:26:38.950
Well, he says a blank sheet of paper equals endless possibilities.

617
00:26:39.170 --> 00:26:40.270
It's conceptual.

618
00:26:40.510 --> 00:26:41.510
That's what he says.

619
00:26:41.850 --> 00:26:45.250
Meanwhile, the booth next door has like a basketball hoop.

620
00:26:45.390 --> 00:26:47.110
And then one has like a popcorn machine.

621
00:26:47.310 --> 00:26:49.510
No one is going to stop by this booth.

622
00:26:49.710 --> 00:26:53.310
And there's a deleted scene, Jenna, that goes on for so long.

623
00:26:53.430 --> 00:27:00.250
I loved it so much of just a steady stream of kids walking past you guys, not stopping

624
00:27:00.250 --> 00:27:00.810
at all.

625
00:27:00.810 --> 00:27:06.550
And the only one that stops briefly sets down his popcorn-like container and just leaves

626
00:27:06.550 --> 00:27:07.650
it there like trash.

627
00:27:07.810 --> 00:27:08.870
It spills over.

628
00:27:09.070 --> 00:27:11.870
And then Oscar is just tidying up this kid's trash.

629
00:27:14.160 --> 00:27:17.300
Angela, we had so much fun shooting these scenes.

630
00:27:17.480 --> 00:27:21.200
I so loved getting to be on location with these three guys.

631
00:27:21.460 --> 00:27:23.300
We had the best time.

632
00:27:23.640 --> 00:27:28.980
They gave all the background actors the direction that they should be like having a great time

633
00:27:28.980 --> 00:27:33.920
and smiling and high energy at all the other booths so that when you got to ours, it was

634
00:27:33.920 --> 00:27:35.840
particularly quiet and sad looking.

635
00:27:36.360 --> 00:27:38.100
It really, really works.

636
00:27:38.300 --> 00:27:39.720
It was cracking me up.

637
00:27:40.060 --> 00:27:43.780
Should we go back over to Dunder Mifflin and see what everyone else is up to?

638
00:27:44.200 --> 00:27:44.640
Yes.

639
00:27:44.800 --> 00:27:46.400
Well, let me tell you what they're up to.

640
00:27:46.520 --> 00:27:48.000
They're up to trying to sneak out of work.

641
00:27:48.240 --> 00:27:50.340
I mean, Meredith and Phyllis are stirring the pot.

642
00:27:50.580 --> 00:27:51.400
Michael is gone.

643
00:27:51.600 --> 00:27:53.100
They don't want to stay the whole day.

644
00:27:53.440 --> 00:27:53.760
No.

645
00:27:53.760 --> 00:27:58.360
And Creed has a great line where he says he finished his work months ago.

646
00:27:58.580 --> 00:27:59.640
I loved that.

647
00:28:00.100 --> 00:28:01.260
Like, what is he doing?

648
00:28:02.060 --> 00:28:05.560
Dwight reminds them all that when Michael is gone, Jim is in charge.

649
00:28:05.760 --> 00:28:07.780
And when Jim is gone, Andy and Dwight are in charge.

650
00:28:07.940 --> 00:28:10.740
And when Andy is gone, you answer to Dwight.

651
00:28:11.020 --> 00:28:13.000
And Stanley's like, I don't give a crap.

652
00:28:13.140 --> 00:28:13.500
I'm out.

653
00:28:13.700 --> 00:28:17.040
I love so much that moment when Stanley walks out the door.

654
00:28:17.340 --> 00:28:17.980
I do too.

655
00:28:18.140 --> 00:28:19.700
And Dwight doesn't know what to do.

656
00:28:19.880 --> 00:28:22.820
You know, we have said we feel like Stanley is the person Dwight is like,

657
00:28:22.820 --> 00:28:25.080
I am not going to, like, mess with Stanley.

658
00:28:25.380 --> 00:28:31.480
But Dwight asserts to the whole office, look, you guys, the American workday ends at five

659
00:28:31.480 --> 00:28:32.020
o'clock.

660
00:28:32.260 --> 00:28:36.120
And so you must stay until the end of the workday, period.

661
00:28:36.520 --> 00:28:37.980
Or I'll tattle on you.

662
00:28:38.480 --> 00:28:38.720
Right.

663
00:28:40.100 --> 00:28:44.120
Back at the high school, Pam is chatting up a young prospective intern.

664
00:28:44.640 --> 00:28:47.680
He's actually interested in Dunder Mifflin.

665
00:28:47.820 --> 00:28:50.540
And Michael signals her to stop.

666
00:28:50.840 --> 00:28:51.160
Yeah.

667
00:28:51.420 --> 00:28:52.560
Well, not just that.

668
00:28:52.560 --> 00:28:54.760
But he goes up to this kid whose name is Justin.

669
00:28:55.140 --> 00:28:57.580
He says, I don't think this is the job for you.

670
00:28:57.800 --> 00:28:59.760
Paper should be more of a hobby for you.

671
00:29:00.040 --> 00:29:03.380
And then he takes away the kid's pamphlet that he was looking at.

672
00:29:03.720 --> 00:29:04.020
Yeah.

673
00:29:04.200 --> 00:29:08.760
We had a fan catch about it, though, from Harmjen V and Alyssa C.

674
00:29:08.760 --> 00:29:18.260
If Michael only brought one sheet of paper to the job fair, where did the brochure come from that Pam gave to Justin and Michael took away?

675
00:29:18.960 --> 00:29:19.420
Hmm.

676
00:29:20.020 --> 00:29:20.540
Good question.

677
00:29:20.780 --> 00:29:22.680
I mean, clearly they brought a brochure.

678
00:29:22.920 --> 00:29:28.760
I felt like the paper was their prop, was the thing that was supposed to be the eye-catching moment as people walked by.

679
00:29:28.760 --> 00:29:32.300
So maybe under the desk, Pam had some additional materials.

680
00:29:32.660 --> 00:29:39.360
When the paper makes you stop in your tracks to talk to these people at their booth, then you get a pamphlet.

681
00:29:39.360 --> 00:29:40.220
Okay.

682
00:29:40.440 --> 00:29:41.080
I like it.

683
00:29:41.860 --> 00:29:47.440
Michael is furious that Justin signed his name on their blank piece of paper.

684
00:29:47.660 --> 00:29:51.520
He's even more furious to learn that Pam only brought the one.

685
00:29:51.980 --> 00:29:52.340
Yeah.

686
00:29:52.440 --> 00:29:54.360
And Pam's like, excuse me?

687
00:29:54.900 --> 00:29:56.820
I love that she totally calls him out on it.

688
00:29:57.040 --> 00:29:57.620
I know.

689
00:29:57.880 --> 00:30:00.060
She said, Michael, do you not remember?

690
00:30:00.540 --> 00:30:04.880
I said, are you sure that we only need one sheet of paper?

691
00:30:05.360 --> 00:30:08.620
And you said, yeah, we only need one sheet of paper to make a difference.

692
00:30:08.840 --> 00:30:12.620
And then you sneezed in my tea and said, don't worry, it's just allergies.

693
00:30:13.400 --> 00:30:14.860
Pam, with the receipts.

694
00:30:15.240 --> 00:30:16.500
That's what the kids say, Jenna.

695
00:30:16.880 --> 00:30:17.580
Oh, I like it.

696
00:30:18.140 --> 00:30:20.000
We had a fan question from Kate B.

697
00:30:20.000 --> 00:30:24.160
When Justin signs his name on the piece of paper, he writes Justin E.

698
00:30:24.440 --> 00:30:29.240
But later in the episode, he tells Michael that his last name is Paul Snick.

699
00:30:29.640 --> 00:30:30.260
Explain.

700
00:30:30.840 --> 00:30:31.100
Hmm.

701
00:30:31.360 --> 00:30:32.240
I'll tell you.

702
00:30:32.420 --> 00:30:33.520
I am intrigued.

703
00:30:33.900 --> 00:30:39.420
The actor who plays Justin, his name is Trevor Einhorn.

704
00:30:39.840 --> 00:30:42.580
Oh, so maybe he was writing his actual last name.

705
00:30:42.880 --> 00:30:43.520
Yes.

706
00:30:43.740 --> 00:30:44.560
Isn't that amazing?

707
00:30:45.000 --> 00:30:45.340
Mm-hmm.

708
00:30:45.340 --> 00:30:50.380
So Trevor Einhorn has been a professional actor since he was five years old.

709
00:30:50.560 --> 00:30:55.260
And his breakout role was on the television comedy, Frasier.

710
00:30:55.480 --> 00:31:00.080
He played Frederick Crane, the son of Frasier and Lilith.

711
00:31:00.300 --> 00:31:01.760
Oh, my gosh.

712
00:31:01.960 --> 00:31:02.400
Yes.

713
00:31:02.680 --> 00:31:05.660
He also went on to have a recurring role on Mad Men.

714
00:31:05.840 --> 00:31:08.700
He was in season six and seven as a copywriter.

715
00:31:08.700 --> 00:31:13.680
And he also played Neil Kellerman in the ABC remake of Dirty Dancing.

716
00:31:14.340 --> 00:31:14.620
Hmm.

717
00:31:14.860 --> 00:31:19.020
He currently stars as Josh Hoberman in the sci-fi show The Magicians.

718
00:31:19.300 --> 00:31:20.640
He is a Scorpio.

719
00:31:20.780 --> 00:31:21.720
I'll have you know.

720
00:31:22.080 --> 00:31:23.260
That's new for a gueSt.

721
00:31:23.340 --> 00:31:26.180
I don't feel like you've shared their zodiac sign before.

722
00:31:26.420 --> 00:31:28.460
Is this a new part of the guest star breakdown?

723
00:31:28.940 --> 00:31:31.500
Well, it showed up in his bio, so I thought I'd throw it in there.

724
00:31:31.760 --> 00:31:31.940
Okay.

725
00:31:31.940 --> 00:31:37.140
Finally, he is not related to our cinematographer, Randall Einhorn.

726
00:31:37.360 --> 00:31:38.340
That is correct.

727
00:31:38.540 --> 00:31:39.760
Just a coincidence.

728
00:31:40.560 --> 00:31:43.980
Everyone back in the bullpen still really wants to sneak out.

729
00:31:44.280 --> 00:31:45.740
They've waited for their moment.

730
00:31:45.960 --> 00:31:49.680
Dwight finally gets up and exits the bullpen to go to the bathroom.

731
00:31:50.120 --> 00:31:52.600
And Creed calls Angela to tell her they're all leaving.

732
00:31:52.940 --> 00:31:57.820
This scene made me laugh so hard, Jenna, because this is a bit that Creed and I still do.

733
00:31:57.820 --> 00:32:02.160
To this day, Creed will text me, Pumpkin's out.

734
00:32:02.660 --> 00:32:05.060
Or if we hang out, I'm like, all right, Creed, I'm going to go.

735
00:32:05.220 --> 00:32:06.260
He'll say, Pumpkin's out.

736
00:32:06.400 --> 00:32:09.960
Like, he still says this to me after all of these years.

737
00:32:10.360 --> 00:32:12.560
And this just warmed my heart.

738
00:32:12.760 --> 00:32:13.620
I loved it so much.

739
00:32:13.740 --> 00:32:14.780
Sam, can you play the clip?

740
00:32:19.510 --> 00:32:20.310
Extension 128.

741
00:32:20.690 --> 00:32:21.410
Hiya, Pumpkin.

742
00:32:21.410 --> 00:32:21.890
It's Creed.

743
00:32:22.550 --> 00:32:23.850
Say we're going to ditch this bitch.

744
00:32:24.090 --> 00:32:24.510
You in?

745
00:32:25.110 --> 00:32:25.510
No.

746
00:32:25.770 --> 00:32:26.310
Are you out?

747
00:32:28.830 --> 00:32:29.430
Pumpkin's out.

748
00:32:29.590 --> 00:32:30.170
Let's go, gang.

749
00:32:31.150 --> 00:32:34.270
We got so much mail about this scene, Angela.

750
00:32:34.650 --> 00:32:39.350
People who listen to the podcast know that Creed calls you Pumpkin in real life.

751
00:32:39.410 --> 00:32:45.150
And they wanted to know, was Creed ad-libbing in the scene and using his real life nickname

752
00:32:45.150 --> 00:32:45.750
for you?

753
00:32:45.910 --> 00:32:47.970
Or was Pumpkin in the script?

754
00:32:48.230 --> 00:32:50.490
And then he started calling you Pumpkin after this.

755
00:32:50.730 --> 00:32:53.490
This scene is exactly as it is in the script.

756
00:32:53.750 --> 00:32:54.190
Exactly.

757
00:32:54.650 --> 00:32:56.610
So Creed did not improvise that.

758
00:32:56.610 --> 00:33:01.030
But I will tell you, ever since we shot Job Fair, he has called me Pumpkin.

759
00:33:01.170 --> 00:33:03.050
To this day, he still calls me Pumpkin.

760
00:33:03.350 --> 00:33:04.450
That is so cute.

761
00:33:04.690 --> 00:33:05.290
I know.

762
00:33:05.290 --> 00:33:06.210
It's so cute.

763
00:33:06.490 --> 00:33:07.710
I loved that scene.

764
00:33:08.150 --> 00:33:12.010
Well, now Dwight comes out of the restroom and he sees that everyone has left.

765
00:33:13.310 --> 00:33:14.770
Everyone except Angela.

766
00:33:15.430 --> 00:33:15.770
Mm-hmm.

767
00:33:16.070 --> 00:33:19.390
